### Option 4: Partial Sanitization (Preserve Some Context)
```rust
fn sanitize_error_message(msg: &str) -> String {
    // Keep first directory level, but hide deep paths
    // /home/user/.inko-tantivy/production/index -> /home/<path>/index
    // /tmp/inko-test/data/segments -> /tmp/<path>/segments

    if let Some(start) = msg.find('/') {
        if let Some(second_slash) = msg[start..].find('/') {
            if let Some(third_slash) = msg[start + second_slash..].find('/') {
                // Keep first two levels, hide rest
                let before = &msg[..start + second_slash + third_slash];
                let after = &msg[start + second_slash + third_slash..];

                // Replace middle part
                format!("{}<path>{}", before, after.split('/').last().unwrap_or(&""))
            } else {
                // Shallow path, keep it
                msg.to_string()
            }
        } else {
            // No second slash, keep it
            msg.to_string()
        }
    } else {
        // No path at all
        msg.to_string()
    }
}
```

**Pros**:
- Preserves some context (directory name)
- Still protects deep structure
- Balances security and debuggability

**Cons**:
- More complex sanitization logic
- Still loses some information
- May be confusing (why this part shown but not that part?)

